from assemble_topic import assemble, W, read, dump
FACTS={
'01':('HIV is the human immunodeficiency virus attacking the immune system; AIDS is associated with the most advanced stage of infection.','HIV status is not a diagnosis of AIDS, a fixed prediction of illness or a measure of a person’s social or economic value.'),
'01-01':('HIV transmission requires relevant exposure and does not occur through ordinary social contact such as shared food or hugs.','Effective treatment can support long healthy lives; maintained undetectable viral load prevents sexual transmission.'),
'01-02':('AIDS is a syndrome rather than a separate virus.','A clinical condition or individual status cannot be inferred from appearance or a regional population statistic.'),
'02':('NAMPHIA 2017 regional prevalence estimates concern the stated adult age group and uncertainty, not current new-infection counts.','Regional prevalence differences need compatible methods and contextual evidence before causal or individual conclusions.'),
'03':('Unmanaged illness, inadequate access and discrimination can create distinct demographic or livelihood pressures.','Effective care and supportive conditions can preserve participation rather than making those pressures inevitable.'),
'03-01':('Premature deaths can affect age structure and survival indicators where relevant health conditions are not effectively managed.','A local age gap alone does not isolate HIV from migration, past births or other causes.'),
'03-02':('Care needs, interrupted schooling or stigma can affect households through specific pathways.','Household circumstances vary and HIV-related categories should not be assigned without appropriate evidence.'),
'03-03':('Illness can reduce paid or unpaid working time and create care or travel costs under stated conditions.','Treatment expenditure has to be considered alongside health, participation and livelihood benefits.'),
'04':('Government, local bodies, NGOs, community organisations, private employers and individuals can support different response functions.','Programme activity, usable access and measured outcomes are separate evidence levels.'),
'04-01':('Government response depends on suitable staff, supplies, planning, standards and maintained service links.','Treatment-cascade percentages require their own stated conditional denominators.'),
'04-02':('Local authorities and NGOs can reduce access barriers through suitable services, outreach and coordination.','Information alone may not address a transport, affordability or supply barrier.'),
'04-03':('Faith/community support and fair private workplaces can reduce exclusion while complementing qualified healthcare.','An individual’s usable choices depend on service availability and conditions as well as information.'),
'05':('Correct consistent condom use can reduce sexual HIV transmission and unintended pregnancy through different mechanisms.','Sustained usable ART supports health and survival rather than acting as a recreational-drug harm.'),
'05-01':('Condom-related effects on births and later mortality can influence different population components.','Distribution totals do not establish actual correct consistent use or a precise national growth effect.'),
'05-02':('ART suppresses HIV rather than curing it and can preserve education, work and care roles when effective treatment is maintained.','Improved survival can raise numbers living with HIV without establishing an increase in new infections.')}
CARDS={
'001':('Name the virus whose full name is human immunodeficiency virus.','HIV.',['1 mark for the virus name HIV.']),
'002':('Describe the distinction in this record: A names the virus HIV; B names AIDS as an advanced-stage syndrome; a person’s present clinical condition is not supplied.','A identifies the virus and B the associated syndrome, rather than two viruses. HIV status alone does not establish AIDS. The record does not diagnose the person’s current condition.',['1 mark for the virus/syndrome relationship; 1 for the status/condition evidence distinction; 1 for the bounded clinical interpretation.']),
'003':('Name the body system HIV attacks.','The immune system.',['1 mark for the system name immune system.']),
'004':('Name what AIDS stands for.','Acquired immunodeficiency syndrome.',['1 mark for the syndrome name in full.']),
'005':('Calculate the difference in percentage points between NAMPHIA 2017 adult (15–64) prevalence point estimates: Zambezi 22.3%, Kunene 7.6%.','22.3−7.6 = 14.7 percentage points. This is a point-estimate difference, not a relative percentage change or a current rate.',['1 mark for the subtraction method; 1 for the answer 14.7; 1 for the percentage-point unit.']),
'006':('Describe the supplied historical NAMPHIA 2017 prevalence chart for adults aged 15–64: Kunene 7.6% (95% CI 4.9–10.3), Khomas 8.3% (6.6–10.1), Ohangwena 17.9% (16.0–19.8), Zambezi 22.3% (17.5–27.0).','The point estimates vary across the named regions, with the two northern/eastern examples higher than the two lower examples shown. Zambezi’s point estimate is highest among these four, but uncertainty overlaps some comparisons. This is historical prevalence, not current incidence or evidence about an individual resident.',['1 mark for the regional pattern; 1 for correct dated chart/uncertainty evidence; 1 for the bounded prevalence interpretation.']),
'007':('Explain how poorly managed illness can affect a household’s resources where it interrupts earnings and adds care costs.','Illness can reduce available working time and earnings while increasing relevant expenses. Less money remains for other needs under those conditions. Effective care, income support and fair access can change the pathway, so the outcome is not inherent to HIV status.',['1 mark for illness/access conditions as a factor; 1 for the income/cost link; 1 for reduced available funds; 1 for the conditional household effect and relevant response.']),
'008':('Describe the hypothetical account: earnings fall from N$3,000 to N$2,000 during an illness interval and care/travel costs add N$400; other flows are unchanged.','Earnings are N$1,000 lower and additional costs N$400 higher, leaving N$1,400 less for other uses. The account describes the supplied household interval, not a typical loss or a personal diagnosis.',['1 mark for the income/cost pattern; 1 for the reconciled N$1,400 evidence; 1 for the bounded interpretation.']),
'009':('Name one demographic indicator affected where preventable illness causes premature deaths.','Mortality, life expectancy or appropriately measured age structure.',['1 mark for a relevant demographic-indicator name.']),
'010':('Name one social barrier distinct from the biological infection that can harm participation or service access.','Stigma or discrimination.',['1 mark for the barrier name stigma/discrimination.']),
'011':('Name an economic pathway through which illness can reduce household resources, without assuming it occurs for everyone.','Interrupted earnings/working time or increased relevant care/travel costs.',['1 mark for a relevant economic-pathway point.']),
'012':('Name one response function requiring qualified health services, beyond a general awareness event.','Appropriate testing, treatment or clinical care.',['1 mark for a relevant service-function name.']),
'013':('How far do you agree that distributing information alone proves an effective HIV response? The record has no usable-access, continued-care or outcome evidence.','Disagree with effectiveness being established. Distribution shows activity, not whether information is accurate, used or connected to needed services. Access barriers and continued care require evidence. Awareness can contribute without proving the whole response succeeded.',['Up to 4 C marks for judgement using the distribution evidence and missing information/use/access/outcome links.','1 C mark for a supported response-effectiveness decision.']),
'014':('How far do you agree that a clinic building proves maintained treatment access? Staff, medicine continuity, affordability and reachable delivery are unrecorded.','Disagree. A building is an enabling asset, while usable maintained care depends on the supplied missing functions. Relevant service and continuity records are needed. The project can have potential without demonstrated access for intended people.',['Up to 4 C marks for judgement using building evidence and the staff/supply/access conditions.','1 C mark for a supported decision about maintained service.']),
'015':('How far do you agree that another leaflet campaign resolves the stated barrier when residents have accurate information but cannot afford transport to the available service?','Disagree for that barrier. Information is already present; the constraint is reaching care. A suitable access or transport response is more directly linked, with feasibility and confidentiality respected. Leaflet totals alone would not establish improved service access.',['Up to 4 C marks for judgement using the information/transport evidence, mechanism fit and relevant feasible response.','1 C mark for a supported barrier-resolution decision.']),
'016':('How far do you agree that a supportive workplace policy proves staff experienced no discrimination? Implementation and confidential experience records are absent.','Disagree. The written policy states an intention, while actual practice remains unmeasured. Appropriate protected evidence and accountable implementation are needed. Supportive employment can help without the policy’s existence establishing every employee’s experience.',['Up to 4 C marks for judgement using the policy/practice evidence distinction and relevant protected follow-up.','1 C mark for a supported decision about demonstrated non-discrimination.']),
'017':('Explain how sustained effective ART can support both survival and household participation.','ART suppresses HIV and supports maintained health when care is effective and continued. Reduced illness can preserve life and time for education, work or care roles. The demographic/economic benefit depends on usable sustained services, not a cure claim.',['1 mark for maintained treatment as a factor; 1 for the suppression/health link; 1 for improved survival; 1 for the conditional participation effect.']),
'018':('Describe this programme pattern: more people survive and live with HIV while new infections fall; the age group and method are comparable.','The living population and new-infection flow move differently. Better survival can help raise numbers living with HIV despite lower incidence. The record does not justify treating prevalence as the same measure as new infections.',['1 mark for the opposing stock/flow pattern; 1 for the comparable survival/incidence evidence; 1 for the indicator distinction.']),
'019':('Name the use condition needed when describing condoms’ protection against sexual HIV transmission.','Correct and consistent use.',['1 mark for the complete use-condition point.']),
'020':('Name the medical treatment suppressing HIV replication rather than curing HIV.','Antiretroviral therapy or ART.',['1 mark for the treatment name ART.']),
'021':('Explain the error in calling AIDS a virus separate from HIV. What distinction tests the terms?','The confusion substitutes syndrome for virus. HIV is the human immunodeficiency virus; AIDS is an advanced-stage syndrome associated with infection. Classifying the named agent versus clinical syndrome provides the test.',['1 mark for the virus/syndrome error; 1 for the correct distinction and discriminating classification test.']),
'022':('Explain the error in applying harmful recreational-drug arguments to prescribed ART in a population-economy discussion.','The confusion transfers a different drug category onto treatment. ART suppresses HIV and, with maintained effective care, supports survival and participation. The test identifies the medicine’s therapeutic role and conditions rather than assuming harm from the word drug.',['1 mark for the category confusion; 1 for the correct therapeutic distinction and mechanism-based test.'])}
TASKS=[
{'objectives':['01','03','05'],'prompt':'(a) Name the virus HIV in full. [1]\n(b) Describe three distinct demographic, social or economic pathways through which unmanaged illness or discrimination can create pressure, keeping effects conditional. [3]\n(c) Name the treatment category ART in full. [1]\n(d) Describe the hypothetical record: comparable new infections fall while improved survival raises the number living with HIV. [2]','answer':'(a) Human immunodeficiency virus. (b) Suitable pathways include premature mortality affecting structure, care/time pressures, interrupted income or discrimination reducing participation; credit three distinct relevant descriptions with conditions. (c) Antiretroviral therapy. (d) The new-infection flow falls while the living stock rises; improved survival can make those patterns coexist.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for the virus name.','(b) 3 A marks for three distinct conditional pathway point descriptions.','(c) 1 A mark for antiretroviral therapy.','(d) 2 B marks for the differing indicators and the supported survival interpretation.'],'split':{'A':5,'B':2},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'A'),('c','Name',1,'A'),('d','Describe',2,'B')]},
{'objectives':['01','04','05'],'prompt':'A hypothetical eligible group contains 100 people living with HIV. Eighty know their status and sixty-four receive treatment. Fifty-eight of those sixty-four have the stated suppression outcome. No outcome for untreated people is supplied.\n(a) Name the treatment category used to suppress HIV. [1]\n(b) Calculate treatment coverage among the eighty diagnosed people. [2]\n(c) Describe three distinct features of the cascade using the correct denominators. [3]\n(d) What judgement is supported about the claim “eighty per cent of all 100 are receiving treatment”? [1]','answer':'(a) Antiretroviral therapy. (b) 64/80 = 0.8, equivalent to 80%. (c) Eighty of 100 know their status; sixty-four of 100 receive treatment; fifty-eight of sixty-four treated have the stated suppression outcome, about 90.6% of that subgroup. (d) Reject the claim: treatment among all 100 is 64%, while 80% uses the diagnosed subgroup denominator.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for ART.','(b) 2 B marks for the diagnosed-group method and correct percentage.','(c) 3 B marks for three distinct cascade patterns with appropriate resource evidence and denominators.','(d) 1 C mark for the supported denominator judgement.'],'split':{'A':1,'B':5,'C':1},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Calculate',2,'B'),('c','Describe',3,'B'),('d','What',1,'C')]},
{'objectives':['02','04','05'],'prompt':'How far do you agree that a higher prevalence figure alone proves HIV prevention failed? NAMPHIA is a dated 2017 Namibian household survey, while prevalence measures people living with HIV rather than only new infections. In the hypothetical later comparison here, methods and age groups match, survival improves and new infections fall. Judge what a prevalence increase alone can establish. [5]','answer':'Disagree. Prevalence is affected by survival and population composition as well as new infections. The supplied later pattern is compatible with improved survival and reduced incidence, so higher prevalence alone cannot prove prevention failure. A useful response evaluation needs dated compatible incidence, mortality, service and population evidence. The 2017 survey must not be treated as today’s unchanging estimate.','guide':['Up to 4 C marks for judgement using the indicator distinction, survival/incidence evidence and dating/comparability conditions.','1 C mark for a supported decision rejecting failure inferred from prevalence alone.'],'split':{'C':5},'parts':[('a','How far do you agree',5,'C')]}
]
